<Doc title="The .bdoc format" subtitle="the rules" category="Reference" id="theFormat">

<Text>A very small subset of XML: elements, attributes, text, comments. No
namespaces, no DTD, no CDATA. Adding a component is adding a file.</Text>

<Table>
<Row head="yes"><Cell>kind</Cell><Cell>case</Cell><Cell>example</Cell></Row>
<Row><Cell>element</Cell><Cell class="serial">PascalCase</Cell><Cell>Doc Section Pinout Pin Bold</Cell></Row>
<Row><Cell>attribute</Cell><Cell class="digital">camelCase</Cell><Cell>title subtitle class from</Cell></Row>
<Row><Cell>class value</Cell><Cell class="digital">camelCase</Cell><Cell>power ground serial audio</Cell></Row>
</Table>

<Note>An element and an attribute are different kinds of thing, and reading one
as the other is the commonest mistake in a hand-written format. Making them
visibly different makes the mistake visible too. The parser does not enforce
the casing - it cannot refuse a document over a style rule - so it is reported
instead, on the toolbar.</Note>

<Section title="Blocks">
<Table>
<Row head="yes"><Cell>element</Cell><Cell>what it is</Cell></Row>
<Row><Cell>Section</Cell><Cell>a heading and what follows it</Cell></Row>
<Row><Cell>Text</Cell><Cell>a paragraph</Cell></Row>
<Row><Cell>List / Item</Cell><Cell>bullets</Cell></Row>
<Row><Cell>Table / Row / Cell</Cell><Cell>a grid. Row head="yes" for the header</Cell></Row>
<Row><Cell>Note / Warn</Cell><Cell>an aside, and the one that matters</Cell></Row>
<Row><Cell>Code</Cell><Cell>inline a span, on its own a panel - see below</Cell></Row>
<Row><Cell>Draw</Cell><Cell>a canvas - see below</Cell></Row>
<Row><Cell>Pinout / Column / Pin</Cell><Cell>a pad table</Cell></Row>
<Row><Cell>Include</Cell><Cell>another document, spliced in</Cell></Row>
</Table>

<Text>Inline, anywhere: <Code>Bold</Code>, <Code>Dim</Code>, <Code>Code</Code>.
That is the whole markup vocabulary, and it is short on purpose.</Text>

<Text>A <Code>Code</Code> on its own line is a PANEL rather than a span:
gruvbox, line numbers, and the same tokenizer the Code view uses, so a snippet
is coloured the way the file it came from is.</Text>

<Code>/* Drives the servo, once the map says which pad. */
static Bool steer(Float32 fraction)
{
    if(!pins::ready())
    {
        return false;   // begin() has not run
    }

    const Int32 us = STEER_CAL_CENTER + (Int32)(fraction * 220.0f);
    servo::writeUs(pins::active().servo, us);
    return true;
}</Code>

<Note>It does NOT wrap. Code is a shape, and a line folded at the panel edge is
a different shape - an eighty-column pin table folded at sixty-two stops being a
table. The page pans in both directions without limit, so a long line is
reachable rather than lost.</Note>
</Section>

<Section title="Include">
<Code><Include file="pinouts/pico2w.bdoc"/></Code>

<Text>C's #include, resolved relative to the file writing it and spliced in at
parse time. There is no second file type: a pinout is a document, and what
makes it a pinout is what is in it.</Text>

<Note>A failed include is drawn where the content would have been - the missing
file, or the parse error inside it with its own line number. Nesting caps at
eight, which catches self-inclusion and every longer loop with one integer.</Note>
</Section>

<Section title="Draw">
<Code><Draw width="620" height="300">
  setColor power
  fillRect 224 28 250 40
  setTextAlign centre
  drawText 176 27 "VCC"
</Draw></Code>

<Table>
<Row head="yes"><Cell>command</Cell><Cell>arguments</Cell></Row>
<Row><Cell>setColor</Cell><Cell>a palette name, a pin class, or #RRGGBB</Cell></Row>
<Row><Cell>setWidth</Cell><Cell>n</Cell></Row>
<Row><Cell>setTextSize</Cell><Cell>n</Cell></Row>
<Row><Cell>setTextAlign</Cell><Cell>left, centre, right</Cell></Row>
<Row><Cell>drawLine</Cell><Cell>x1 y1 x2 y2</Cell></Row>
<Row><Cell>drawRect / fillRect</Cell><Cell>x1 y1 x2 y2</Cell></Row>
<Row><Cell>drawCircle / fillCircle</Cell><Cell>x y r</Cell></Row>
<Row><Cell>drawTriangle / fillTriangle</Cell><Cell>x1 y1 x2 y2 x3 y3</Cell></Row>
<Row><Cell>drawText</Cell><Cell>x y "words"</Cell></Row>
</Table>

<Text>A line starting # is a comment. Coordinates are the document's own -
Draw declares its natural size and the renderer scales it, so nothing here
ever deals in pixels.</Text>

<Note>An unknown command is reported under the drawing rather than ignored. A
picture quietly missing a wire is worse than one that says which line it could
not read.</Note>
</Section>

<Section title="On screen">
<Text>The wheel zooms and dragging pans, and both act on the whole page - text
and drawings scale by the same number. The toggle above the editor swaps
between the page and the source that made it.</Text>
</Section>

</Doc>
